          "reasoning_tokens": 0,
          "finish_reason": "length",
          "answer_excerpt": "# Technical Analysis of Plant Condition Monitoring Records (Records 0000000\u20130000017)\n\n## 1. Scope and Method\n\nThis report analyzes 18 condition-monitoring records spanning six asset classes (valves, pumps, separators, heat exchangers, reactors, scrubbers, condensers, and one compressor) across 17 stations. Each record contains five measured parameters: pressure (kPa), temperature (\u00b0C), flow (m\u00b3/h), vibration (mm/s RMS), and efficiency (%).\n\nBecause the dataset contains no nameplate data, design duty points, or historical baselines, this analysis proceeds by:\n\n1. **Cross-population benchmarking** \u2014 comparing each unit against the statistical distribution of the full record set to flag outliers.\n2. **Asset-class reasoning** \u2014 applying known failure modes for each equipment type to interpret the measured signature.\n3. **Cross-parameter consistency checks** \u2014 identifying physically implausible or degraded combinations (e.g., high vibration with low flow, high temperature with low efficiency).\n\nKey population statistics used throughout:\n\n| Parameter | Min | Max | Approx. Median | Approx. Mean |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Pressure (kPa) | 116 | 764 | ~465",
